<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">New Orleans</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1718">1718</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">New Orleans is a port city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi River</a> near its mouth, founded in 1718 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-baptiste-le-moyne-de-bienville">Jean-Baptiste Le Moyne de Bienville</a>. It became the capital of French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a>, passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1763 and returned briefly to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana-purchase">Louisiana Purchase</a> of 1803. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">Creole</a> culture and river trade made it the commercial centre of the Gulf coast.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/new-orleans.jpg" alt="New Orleans" width="320" height="242" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: George Bannister · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:New_Orleans_from_the_Air_September_2019_-_Central_Business_District_Skyline_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>New Orleans lies in the Mississippi River Delta, south of Lake Pontchartrain, on the banks of the Mississippi about 105 miles upriver from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-mexico">Gulf of Mexico</a>. It was originally settled on the river's natural levees, the high ground, and after the Flood Control Act of 1965 the Army Corps of Engineers built levees around a much larger area that included former marshland. Half of the city is now at or below local mean sea level. Before European colonists arrived, the Choctaw called the area Bulbancha, meaning land of many tongues.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>French colonists founded the city in 1718, and it was the territorial capital of French Louisiana. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-treaty-of-san-ildefonso">Third Treaty of San Ildefonso</a> in 1800 restored French control of New Orleans and Louisiana, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> sold both to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana-purchase">Louisiana Purchase</a> in 1803. The city then grew rapidly with Americans, French, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">Creoles</a> and Africans, and later with Irish, Germans, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> and Italians. Sugar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> were cultivated with slave labour on large plantations nearby.</p><p>In the last campaign of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-1812">War of 1812</a> a British force of 11,000 tried to capture the city, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrew-jackson">Andrew Jackson</a>, leading militia, regulars and local privateers under Jean Lafitte, defeated it in the Battle of New Orleans on 8 January 1815. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-ghent">Treaty of Ghent</a> had been signed already, but neither army had learned of it.</p><p>In April 1862 the Union Navy occupied the city, and General Benjamin F. Butler became military governor. After the war a legislative amendment of 1889 with a grandfather clause effectively disfranchised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freedmen">freedmen</a>, and public schools remained segregated until 1960. The city was the fifth-largest in the country in 1860, but after the mid-19th century growth shifted elsewhere as railways and highways reduced river traffic.</p><p>On 29 August 2005 the federal levee system failed during Hurricane Katrina, and about 80 percent of the city flooded. More than 1,300 people died, thousands of residents were displaced, and the population fell by over 50 percent.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>New Orleans is a consolidated city and parish, coextensive with Orleans Parish, and the most populous city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a>, with 383,997 inhabitants at the 2020 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. It remains a major port and commercial hub for the Gulf Coast, with a maritime industry and petrochemical production nearby. The French Quarter, its historic heart, is known for French and Spanish Creole architecture. The city is known for its music, Creole cuisine and the Mardi Gras celebrations, and its strong Roman Catholic tradition goes back to its French and Spanish settlement.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “New Orleans” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1862 <b>New Orleans</b> fell on 29 April, and the two invasions of Kentucky and Maryland ended at Antietam, which Allan Nevins calls the strategic high-water mark.</blockquote><a href="/empire/confederate-states-of-america">Confederate States of America · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Posts and missions such as Detroit, founded in 1701, Mobile and <b>New Orleans</b>, founded in 1718, became lasting cities.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-france">New France · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Between 1791 and 1810 more than 25,000 Creoles, planters, petits blancs and free people of colour with their slaves, fled to the United States, Jamaica and Cuba; more than 10,000, expelled from Cuba, reached <b>New Orleans</b> in 1809–1810, nearly doubling its population.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Province of Carolina, French <b>New Orleans</b> from 1718 and Georgia from 1732 hemmed in its borders.</blockquote><a href="/empire/spanish-florida">Spanish Florida · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In the early 20th century Haitian migrants took it to eastern Cuba, and later migrants to the United States, where it drew African Americans and Caribbean migrants, mixed with Santería and Spiritism, and fed the <b>New Orleans</b> Voodoo Revival.</blockquote><a href="/religion/haitian-vodou">Haitian Vodou · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>In <b>New Orleans</b> in 1860 it was the hit of the Rumsey and Newcomb Minstrels, and Mrs. John Wood's singing in a burlesque drew seven encores; the publisher P. P. Werlein printed a version without the dialect.</blockquote><a href="/songs/dixie">Dixie</a></li></ul></section>
